we know next to nothing about elden ring  but that won't stop me and the game spot  staff from listing off some things we'd  love to see in from software's next  outing well i guess i should back up a  little you might know something  but we are going to pretend like that  little leak never happened just to make  it crystal clear  i won't be commenting on the leak or  showing any elden ring gameplay  footage so if you're worried about  spoilers you can rest  easy if you want to see more videos like  this make sure to subscribe and like the  video  it really does help us out  number one world diversity at this point  from software has shown it can execute  spectacularly on different flavors of  fantasy whether it's the medieval vibes  of demon souls and dark souls the cosmic  horror of bloodborne or the sengoku  samurai era of sakura  eldon ring is the studio's first open  world game  and naturally i expect it to be much  grander in scope  the so-called elden ring that has been  shattered is described as something that  commanded the stars and its destruction  burned the sky it would be fascinating  to see the impact this has on numerous  different kinds of societies and  civilizations  in game that would mean being able to  travel from one kingdom to the next  effectively letting from software flex  its creative muscles with a variety of  different themes and aesthetics  using the studio's existing games  imagine traveling from lordran to  yharnam and then to drangleic or  bulletaria and then to ashina  sure it's ambitious but if anyone can  pull it off it's from  software couple that with game of  thrones writer george r martin being  involved in the creation of eldon ring  and you've got a good opportunity to  deliver a vast and diverse world rich in  detail  intricate history and fascinating lore  [Music]  number two a dangerous open world all  that said i've been having a lot of  trouble wrapping my head  around an open world souls-like game i'm  confident it can  work i just don't know how it will work  the maze-like interconnected passageways  have long been a staple of the series  designing an open world game is another  beast entirely will the entire world be  open to you from the get-go  if so how will it be balanced will some  sections of the map be too tough for you  early on or will enemies scale with you  as you level up will some areas be shut  off entirely or will they be gated by  environmental hazards akin to death  mountain and the legend of zelda breath  of the wild either way  the world needs to feel dangerous and  alive no matter where you are  in it number three with caves and  dungeons to explore while i'm excited to  see what from software does with an open  world i hope i still get slices of their  brilliant linear level design  if we do i imagine this will come in the  form of caves  dungeons and castles the player is free  to explore  i want to hop on my horse ride until i  see an abandoned castle  and explore a tightly crafted dungeon  with worthwhile loot  tough enemies and of course a terrifying  boss  number four puzzles speaking of caves  and dungeons hidetaka miyazaki wears his  influences on his sleeve and he is known  to be a big fan  of the legend of zelda franchise in his  own words the franchise was a monumental  part of his younger life and has  acknowledged that zelda became a  textbook for the 3d action games  it would be great to see miyazaki pull  more directly from nintendo's franchise  specifically breath of the wild shrines  outside of gimmick bosses  traditional puzzle solving hasn't been a  big part of from software's games  and although it doesn't need to be the  main focus i would love to see from  software invite the player to think in  different ways in one of their games  perhaps in elden ring the challenges  don't just have to be powerful enemies  but could also include puzzles that can  only be surmounted with analysis and  logical thinking the open world will no  doubt be filled with caves dungeons and  other nooks and crannies to explore and  they'd be perfect for a bit of puzzle  gameplay  number 5 class based design i loved  sekiro  hell it might even be one of my favorite  games of this generation  however i think i speak for most of us  when i say that i miss the class system  found in demon cells dark souls and  bloodborne  if eldon ring is from software's first  open world game a class and leveling  system would feel right at home  i want to be able to test out different  builds level up unique weapons and  create my perfect hero for whatever  terrifying bosses i might face off  against  hopefully from software we'll also  introduce some classes unique to the  world miyazaki and martin have created  i always loved how bloodborne's classes  were wholly unique to its setting and i  hope we get some outlandish builds i can  play around with  or just let me be giant dad again  number six better stealth let's face it  the stealth in dark souls and bloodborne  has always been a bit  awkward and a tad broken  they did a better job of balancing it  over the years but stealth never really  felt like a viable option this changed  with 2019 sekiro shadows die twice  surprisingly the addition of stealth  mechanics didn't make it any easier  either you could even stealth attack  some bosses  but if you didn't have a good grasp of  the combat itself the boss would make  short work of you for elden ring i hope  that stealth becomes a more feasible  option whether that means giving us a  dedicated crouch button or an entire  stealth attribute i can invest in  is another story the key here though is  to balance it i wouldn't want it to feel  cheesy or significantly easier than  other play styles though magic based  gameplay has been present  in some form or another in every souls  game it's never been outstanding in any  demon souls is often remembered as  having the best magic mechanics but  that's usually because it's incredibly  overpowered  not only could you pick off enemies from  a distance with ease but mp restoration  items were common this allowed for a  wide variety of magic  builds that could easily break the  balance in every subsequent souls game  the magic system was reworked entirely  dark souls 3 borrowed a lot from demon  souls magic based mechanics but  none have been as fun or as interesting  as the original demon soul system  hopefully the success of the remake  means from software will take another  look at its magic system  who knows maybe they're cooking up  something else entirely for magic  number eight towns and more npcs one of  my  favorite things about dark souls  bloodborne and sekiro are the memorable  npcs  like many of you i cling on to nearly  every word they say  listening for hints secrets and bits of  lore however  npcs can be scarce some can even be  missed  entirely if you aren't paying attention  i would be really curious to see what  from software would do with a town  populated by npcs  demon souls dark souls and bloodborne  all took place after a catastrophic  event that left most the population  either dead  or hiding hopefully elden ring will show  us a world slightly more alive than  before  also i'm thinking it you're thinking it  we're all thinking it but what role will  patches play in elden ring  sound off in the comments below with  your theories  number 9 dragon rider from software  seems to have a bit of an antagonistic  relationship with dragons and by  extension the players of its games end  up having that too  the mythical beasts are a mainstay in  most of its games and for the most part  players  are either jabbing swords into their  hides  or running frantically away from them as  they fuse spire in our direction  usually on some sort of bridge unless of  course it's that no-good betrayer seat  the scaleless then they're running  frantically away from ice crystals  i think it's time to rewrite that  relationship a bit and let players  befriend a dragon  people have been making a big deal about  elden ring having horse mounted  traversal but why not go one step  further from software let us ride a  dragon  it doesn't have to be all the time and  it doesn't even have to be with our  direct control  maybe there's a kingdom of dragons on  the outskirts of the world and if you  wanted to travel across the land mass  quickly they'd let you take a dragon  as opposed to directly controlling the  beast it could be kind of like world of  warcraft's fast travel system where you  just watch the dragon fly over the  entire world and it just drops you where  you want  then like world of warcraft they could  update the game so that you could play  peggle while on the dragon  on second thought maybe no peggle  especially if it's that wretched peggle  blast  [Music]  number 10 intricate and obtuse lore for  many  the story and lore of the soulsborne  games are the highlights  while some might consider the plots  themselves to be simple there is an  endless amount of backstory to dig into  any one of these games  sekuro on the other hand is a bit of an  exception while there still is a ton of  interesting lore it puts a  greater emphasis on the plot now this  isn't a bad thing by any stretch but  part of the reason why dark souls  bloodborne and demon souls are so  memorable is how  obtuse everything was not only did you  have to work hard to beat bosses but you  had to work hard to unpack the story for  eldon ring i hope from software manages  to find a rewarding balance between an  interesting plot and an intricate and  deep  world those are just a few things  i'd love to see in elden ring let us  know what you think in the comments  below and if you enjoyed this video  don't forget to subscribe thanks all and  i will see you next time  [Music]  you
 

